# shellcheck shell=bash
# nix-direnv is recommended for evaluation caching only. It is NOT required for
# GC safety: direnv's own `use_flake` runs `nix print-dev-env --profile
# "$(direnv_layout_dir)/flake-profile"`, and a Nix profile generation is a
# permanent GC root (`createGeneration` -> `addPermRoot`), so the devShell's
# store paths survive `nix-collect-garbage` with or without nix-direnv. Do not
# add a `nix_direnv_version` probe to solve a problem that does not exist.
#
# Interactive shells enter the devShell on `cd`, after a one-time `direnv
# allow`. Non-interactive contexts — CI, agents, editor tasks, `bash -c` — do
# not fire the prompt hook and must wrap commands explicitly:
#     nix develop --command prek run --all-files
#     direnv exec . <cmd>
use flake

# Route each rustc call through the kache of the host. KACHE_DISABLED=1 runs one
# command without it. CI enters the devshell without direnv, so CI builds
# without kache (project-platform:a-rust-project-builds-through-the-host-compile-cache).
export RUSTC_WRAPPER=kache

# The workspace builds the `exobrain` CLI into target/debug, and this
# repository runs its own build rather than a released pin.
PATH_add target/debug

# Developer-specific values, such as the release-bot App ID and the path of its
# private key, live outside the checkout, in one file that every worktree
# shares. programming/project-bootstrap/03-local-dev-environment.md,
# "Developer-specific values", states the convention, and
# project-env.example is the tracked template. The test skips a file that
# the shell cannot read, such as under a sandbox, and exits 0.
project_env=$HOME/.config/project-env/exobrain-tech.env
if [ -r "$project_env" ]; then source_env "$project_env"; fi

# Report a store copy of this checkout once a day: a flake reference that does
# not read the git tree copies the whole tree into the Nix store
# (project-platform:a-flake-is-read-from-its-git-tree). The same run warns when
# target/ holds more than 10 GB of its own data
# (project-platform:a-large-build-folder-gets-a-warning). A fresh checkout with
# no build and no exobrain on the host skips the guard.
#
# Warn when the nixpkgs pin of flake.lock is over 30 days old, or when an input
# pinned at a version tag, such as release-kit, has a newer release. The check
# moves nothing; each warning names the command that moves the pin.
if command -v exobrain >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  exobrain store-guard --daily || true
  exobrain outdated || true
fi
